the economic policy uncertainty index is  a measure of policy uncertainty so how  uncertain people are about what the  government is going to do in the u.s.  going forward so are they uncertain  about taxes about spending about  healthcare regulation and to measure  this we use a combination of how  frequently the newspapers so paper's  like the Wall Street Journal and The New  York Times discuss economic policy  uncertainty we also look at the amount  of tax code about to expire and finally  we look at how much disagreement there  is amongst economic forecasters about  future taxes and spending and when you  combine these three measures into one  index you get our economic policy  uncertainty index the key thing this  shows is a huge increase in policy  uncertainty since 2008 so before then it  was bouncing up and down occasional  spikes from wars and elections and then  suddenly since 2008 one it's gone up  massively and it's now incredibly high  for the last four years  the level really from 08 onwards is  about as high as the height at 2001  after the 911 terrorist attack so that  was an era of incredible uncertainty who  did the terrorist attacks remembering  that you know bin Laden didn't initially  own up to them the anthrax the war in  Afghanistan is such a huge uncertainty  lasting for 23 months that dropped back  down again now we've seen a similar  levels staying that way for four years  because of things like the debt ceiling  dispute tarp healthcare regulation so  really a new high level that's about as  high as we've ever seen before why does  policy uncertainty so high right now I  think you have to go back a bit in time  to understand it so the story is the US  has been slowly running a deficit it's  really spending more than his earned  that was disguised in the mid-2000s  because we had a booming economy and  very high tax take it's kind of like  you're spending more money than he can  afford but you've had bonuses for the  last four years and it's disguised the  fact you're running a deficit suddenly  the booms gone away and it's become  painfully clear we're spending far too  much money compared to what are raising  in taxes so what that's done is forced  attention on to how can we a cut  spending and be put up taxes now the  political parties don't agree on how to  do that the Republicans primarily on a  cut expenditure and the Democrats  primarily want to put up taxes and they  don't agree in there you know innocence  they really don't agree they have really  very polarized opposite opinions and as  they fight between taxes up versus  spending down it generates tremendous  uncertainty in the economy  why does policy uncertainty matter  policy uncertainty generates a slowdown  and the reason is businesses tend to  wait on making expense of hiring in  investment decisions when they're  uncertain so if I'm running let's say  sandwich shop in Palo Alto around you  know in California I'm thinking of  opening up a second branch go making  money but I'm very uncertain I may wait  I may wait to see what happens to policy  next year before I make my decision now  of course if everybody waits to her at  best you have a recession how long will  policy on certain you go on for how long  are we going to see incredible levels of  policy uncertainty my view is this is  going to last for some time and the  reason is the big driver is the fact  that the deficit is in such a mess we  spend so much more than they earn and  that's driven by huge spending on health  care so we look at where America's  spendings going up and up and up it's on  healthcare expenditure and I don't see  that changing very radically so unless  we get health care under control we're  going to keep running deficits and keep  having crisis every few years  you
 

